--TEST--
`Core\Command::help` names each parameter's declared type and the default beside it, and names a flag's type alone because an unwritten flag is `false` whatever its declaration says — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The table carries what a parameter is *called* and the conversion the checker
// picked for it, never the type it was declared at. So every type below reached
// the page through the handler's own signature, by the `Class::method` label
// the row already holds — one source of truth, and no second copy of a
// signature in the table.
class Fetcher {
    #[Core\Command(name: "fetch", about: "Download one thing")]
    public static function fetch(
        string $url = "https://example.test",
        #[Core\Option(about: "How many times to retry")] uint $retries = 3,
        #[Core\Option(short: "v")] bool $verbose = false,
    ): uint {
        return 0;
    }
}

// Three declarations and three different answers in the same column:
//
//   * `$url` is a positional carrying a default, so it is bracketed in the
//     usage line and names both halves of its declaration below.
//   * `$retries` is the § 6 example, and `uint` is the half a matcher's own
//     conversion could not have said: `int` and `uint` are one arm apart in
//     the table and one word apart in the declaration.
//   * `$verbose` is a flag. It names `bool` and *not* `bool = false`, because
//     § 6 gives an option declared `bool` by its being written — a page
//     printing the declaration's default there would name a value the matcher
//     does not hand the handler.
echo "" . Core\Command::help("fetch");
--EXPECT--
usage: fetch [<url>] [--retries] [--verbose]

Download one thing

arguments:
  <url>  string = https://example.test

options:
  --retries      uint = 3  How many times to retry
  -v, --verbose  bool
